-### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-*   **Memory Footprint:** Performance depends heavily on grid resolution; high resolutions lead to large memory usage [S3].
-*   **Near-Camera Floaters:** Overfitting near the camera can cause artifacts. Mitigated by transmittance-based gradient scaling [S2].
-*   **Overfitting:** High-frequency SH components can lead to inaccurate geometry. Mitigated by curriculum training (starting with lower SH degree) [S2].
-*   **Bank Conflicts:** In GPU implementations, accessing SH coefficients can cause shared memory bank conflicts [S5, unofficial].
-*   **Clamping:** Color values are clamped to non-negative ranges, which affects gradient flow [S5, unofficial].
+
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+*   **Memory Footprint:** Performance depends excessively on spatial resolution, leading to large memory usage [S3].
+*   **Bank Conflicts:** In GPU implementations, accessing SH basis values can cause bank conflicts [S5, unofficial].
+*   **Clamping:** Color accumulation uses `fmaxf` to clamp to non-negative values, which affects gradient computation [S5, unofficial].
+*   **Overfitting:** Near-camera floaters can occur due to gradient imbalance; transmittance-based gradient scaling is recommended [S2].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Speed:** Training is significantly faster than NeRF (two orders of magnitude) due to the elimination of MLP inference [S1][S5, unofficial]. Rendering can be real-time on consumer hardware [S1].
-*   **Memory:** Higher memory consumption than neural network-based methods due to the explicit grid storage [S1][S5, unofficial].
-*   **Quality:** Comparable to NeRF on standard datasets, but may struggle with very high-frequency details without high grid resolutions [S2].
-*   **Scalability:** Scales with grid resolution; sparse implementations help mitigate memory issues [S2][S5, unofficial].
-*   **Use Cases:** Fast training and real-time rendering applications where high accuracy is required but neural network inference is too slow [S1][S5, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Rendering is $O(N)$ per ray, where $N$ is the number of samples. Training is significantly faster than NeRF (two orders of magnitude) due to the elimination of MLP inference [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(R^3)$ for a grid of resolution $R$, as it stores explicit values for every voxel in the bounding box [S3]. This is higher than hash-based or tree-based methods for high resolutions.
+*   **Performance:** On a GTX 1060, CPU-based ray marching with 100 bins took ~1.4s (OpenMP 4 threads) for a single image [S7, unofficial]. GPU implementations are much faster.
+*   **Strengths:** Fast training and rendering, simple implementation, no need for neural network libraries.
+*   **Limitations:** High memory consumption at high resolutions, limited view-dependent detail compared to higher-order SH or neural networks, requires careful tuning of grid resolution.
 
 ## Background
-Plenoxels was introduced by Fridovich-Keil et al. in 2022 [S5, unofficial]. It was designed to address the computational inefficiency of NeRF by replacing the neural network with an explicit voxel grid representation [S1, S2]. The method leverages spherical harmonics to encode view-dependent appearance, allowing for fast evaluation via trilinear interpolation [S1, S3]. It has since been extended with techniques like depth-guided pruning and curriculum training to improve performance in few-shot settings [S2].
+Plenoxels was introduced by Sara Fridovich-Keil, Alex Yu, Matthew Tancik, et al. in 2022 at CVPR [S5, unofficial]. It was proposed as a direct replacement for the neural network in NeRF, using an explicit voxel grid with spherical harmonics to model radiance fields. The method was validated on synthetic (Blender) and real-world (LLFF, Tanks and Temples) datasets [S2, S3]. It builds on the idea of explicit radiance fields but focuses on efficiency by avoiding neural network inference [S2].
 
